1. Calculate the time difference between the current time and the time when the enemy is expected to reach the interpolated position. This will give you an estimate of how much time the enemy has left to reach the target position.
2. Calculate the distance between the enemy's current position and the interpolated position. This will give you an estimate of how far the enemy is from the target position.
3. Use the time difference and distance to calculate the likelihood of the enemy following the path. A higher time difference and lower distance will indicate a higher likelihood of the enemy following the path.
4. Use the likelihood of the enemy following the path to calculate the hitchance. A higher likelihood will result in a higher hitchance, while a lower likelihood will result in a lower hitchance.
5. Convert the hitchance value to a percentage between 0.1 and 1. A hitchance of 1 indicates that the enemy is unlikely to miss the spell, while a hitchance of 0.1 indicates that the enemy is very unlikely to miss the spell.

Here's an example of code that implements this hitchance system:
```csharp
// Calculate the time difference between the current time and the expected time of arrival at the interpolated position
float timeDifference = (enemyPath[1].t - currentTime) / enemyPath[1].duration;

// Calculate the distance between the current position and the interpolated position
