Sure, here's an example of a C function that takes a `color` struct and an array of `color` structs, and returns the index of the closest match in the array:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

// Define the color struct
typedef struct color {
    int r;
    int g;
    int b;
} color;

// Function to find the closest color in an array
int find_closest_color(color array[], int num_colors, color target) {
    // Initialize variables for closest color and distance
    int closest_index = -1;
    float closest_distance = 100;

    // Iterate through each color in the array
    for (int i = 0; i < num_colors; i++) {
        // Calculate the distance between the target color and the current color
        float distance = sqrt(pow(target.r - array[i].r, 2) + pow(target.g - array[i].g, 2) + pow(target.b - array[i].b, 2));

        // If the current color is closer than the current closest color, update the variables
        if (distance < closest_distance) {
            closest_index = i;
            closest_distance = distance;
        }
    }

    // Return the index of the closest color
    return closest_index;
}

int main() {
    // Define an array of colors
    color array[7] = {
        {0, 0, 0},
        {255, 0, 0},
        {0, 255, 0},
        {0, 0, 255},
        {255, 255, 0},
        {255, 0, 255},
        {0, 255, 255}
    };

    // Define a target color
    color target = {128, 128, 128};

    // Find the index of the closest color in the array
    int closest_index = find_closest_color(array, 7, target);

    // Print the result
    printf("The closest color to %d, %d, %d is index %d\n", target.r, target.g, target.b, closest_index);

    return 0;
}
```
This function first calculates the distance between the target color and each color in the array, then finds the index of the color with the smallest distance and returns it.
You can change the sqrt to a different mathematical function for a different distance calculation

Here is an example of how the proxy pattern and the thread-safe singleton pattern could be implemented in Java:

1. Proxy Pattern:
```
public interface Proxy {
  // This is the interface that the proxy will implement
}

public class ProxyImplementation implements Proxy {
  // This is the concrete implementation of the proxy
}

public class RealObject {
  // This is the object that the proxy is representing
}

public class Client {
  public void useProxy() {
    // This is the client code that uses the proxy
  }
}
```
2. Thread-safe Singleton Pattern:
```
public class Singleton {
  private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();

  private Singleton() {}

  public static Singleton getInstance() {
    return INSTANCE;
  }
}

public class Client {
  public void useSingleton() {
    // This is the client code that uses the singleton
  }
}
```
To use the proxy pattern and thread-safe singleton pattern together, you could create a proxy that wraps the singleton instance. For example:
```
public class Proxy {
  private static final Singleton INSTANCE = Singleton.getInstance();

  private Proxy() {}

  public static Proxy getInstance() {
    return INSTANCE;
  }

  // This is the interface that the proxy will implement
  // The methods in this interface will be implemented by the proxy
}

public class Client {
  public void useProxy() {
    Proxy proxy = Proxy.getInstance();
    // This is the client code that uses the proxy
  }
}
```
Note: This is just one example of how the proxy pattern and thread-safe singleton pattern could be implemented in Java. There are many other ways to do it.

