Sure! Here is an example of a simple NFT minter in Solidity, which will mint a single NFT with a given name and symbol. The code assumes the use of an ERC-721 standard for representing NFTs on the Ethereum blockchain.

First, we need to define a contract that will hold the functions for minting the NFTs:
```
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract NFTMinter {
    // Mapping of NFT names to their corresponding symbol
    mapping (string => string) public symbols;

    // Mapping of NFT symbols to their corresponding names
    mapping (string => string) public nameSymbols;

    // Function to mint a new NFT
    function mint(string memory _name, string memory _symbol) public {
        // Ensure that the name is not empty
        require(_name!= "");
        // Ensure that the symbol is not empty
        require(_symbol!= "");

        // Add the name and symbol to the mapping of NFT names and symbols
        symbols[_name] = _symbol;
        nameSymbols[_symbol] = _name;
    }
}
```
Now, we can use this contract to mint new NFTs. For example, the following code can be used to mint a new NFT called "Example NFT" with the symbol "EXAMPLE":
```
// Create a new contract instance
NFTMinter contractMinter = NFTMinter(0x01);

// Mint a new NFT
contractMinter.mint("Example NFT", "EXAMPLE");
```
This will create a new NFT with the name "Example NFT" and the symbol "EXAMPLE" in the contract's mappings. Note that the `mint` function does not add the NFT to the blockchain, it only updates the internal state of the contract.

