The n-th Fibonacci number is a mathematical sequence where each number is the sum of the previous two numbers. Here's a method to calculate the n-th Fibonacci number in C#:

1. Define a class for the Fibonacci sequence:
```csharp
public class Fibonacci
{
    public int FibonacciNumber { get; set; }

    public Fibonacci(int fibonacciNumber)
    {
        FibonacciNumber = fibonacciNumber;
    }
}
```
2. Create a method that takes the n-th Fibonacci number as an input:
```csharp
public static Fibonacci Fibonacci(int n)
{
    if (n == 0)
    {
        return new Fibonacci(0);
    }
    else if (n == 1)
    {
        return new Fibonacci(1);
    }
    else
    {
        int previousFibonacciNumber = 0;
        int currentFibonacciNumber = 1;
        int result = 0;

        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++)
        {
            result = previousFibonacciNumber + currentFibonacciNumber;
            previousFibonacciNumber = currentFibonacciNumber;
            currentFibonacciNumber = result;
        }

        return new Fibonacci(result);
    }
}
```
3. Test the method with different inputs:
```csharp
int input = 10;
Fibonacci fibonacci = Fibonacci(input);
Console.WriteLine("The n-th Fibonacci number is: " + fibonacci.FibonacciNumber);
```
This code will output: "The n-th Fibonacci number is: 55".

Sure, here's an example of a CUDA kernel that performs bilinear sampling from an image:

```scss
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__global__ void bilinear_sampling(unsigned char *output, int output_width, int output_height,
                                  const unsigned char *input, int input_width, int input_height,
                                  int tex_width, int tex_height, int tex_min, int tex_max) {
    int tx = threadIdx.x;
    int ty = threadIdx.y;
    int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + tx;
    int j = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + ty;

    int x = (i / tex_width) * tex_width * 2;
    int y = (j / tex_height) * tex_height * 2;
    int pixel_value = input[i * input_width + j * input_height / 2];

    unsigned char r = pixel_value * tex_max / tex_width;
    unsigned char g = (pixel_value * tex_max / tex_width) * tex_height;
    unsigned char b = (pixel_value * tex_max / tex_width) * tex_height;

    output[i * output_width + j * 2] = r;
    output[i * output_width + j * output_width + 1] = g;
    output[i * output_width + j * output_width + 2] = b;
}
```

This kernel takes an input image represented as 8-bit unsigned char values, and performs bilinear sampling on each pixel to produce a color value. The output image is represented as 24-bit unsigned char values, with each pixel consisting of three RGB values. The kernel uses thread-level parallelism to perform the bilinear sampling operation for each pixel in the image.

Here is an example of a webpage that loads the horse model from Three.js examples:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Three.js Horse Model Example</title>
    <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/three.js/r129/three.min.js"></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="container"></div>
    <script>
      // Create a scene
      const scene = new THREE.Scene();

      // Create a camera
      const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight, 0.1, 1000);

      // Create a renderer
      const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer();
      renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
      document.getElementById("container").appendChild(renderer.domElement);

      // Load the horse model
      const loader = new THREE.GLTFLoader();
      loader.load("https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/three.js/r129/examples/js/horse.gltf", function (gltf) {
        scene.add(gltf.scene);
      });

      // Render the scene
      function animate() {
        requestAnimationFrame(animate);
        renderer.render(scene, camera);
      }
      animate();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```
This webpage uses Three.js to load the horse model from the Three.js examples, and adds it to the scene. The `animate` function is called repeatedly to render the scene in the renderer.

Here's an example of a thin wrapper for RediSearch using the `redis-py` library that supports CRUD and search:
```python
from redis import Redis
from redis_search.redis_search import RedisSearch

class RediSearch:
    def __init__(self, redis_host='localhost', redis_port=6379, db=0):
        self.redis = Redis(host=redis_host, port=redis_port, db=db)
        self.redis_search = RedisSearch(self.redis)

    def create_index(self, index_name):
        self.redis_search.create_index(index_name)

    def add_document(self, index_name, document_type, id, document_data):
        self.redis_search.add_document(index_name, document_type, id, document_data)

    def get_document(self, index_name, document_type, id):
        return self.redis_search.get_document(index_name, document_type, id)

    def delete_document(self, index_name, document_type, id):
        self.redis_search.delete_document(index_name, document_type, id)

    def search_documents(self, index_name, query, limit=10):
        return self.redis_search.search_documents(index_name, query, limit=limit)
```
This code creates a RediSearch class that wraps around the `redis-py` library and `redis-py-search` library, which provides support for Redis Search. It initializes the Redis connection and the Redis Search index, and provides methods for creating an index, adding documents, getting documents, deleting documents, and searching documents.

