```csharp
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Windows.Forms;
```
2. Declare a variable to hold the `User32` object:
```csharp
[DllImport("user32.dll")]
private static extern IntPtr GetForegroundWindow();

private User32 user32;
```
3. Create a method to get the `User32` object:
```csharp
private void GetUser32()
{
    user32 = (User32)GetForegroundWindow();
}
```
4. Call the `GetUser32()` method:
```csharp
GetUser32();
```
5. Use the `user32` object to access the functions from user32.dll:
```csharp
user32.ShowWindow(int nCmdShow);
```
Note: Keep in mind that you need to handle the cases when the `GetForegroundWindow()` method returns `IntPtr.Zero`, which means that no visible window is currently active. Also, the functions from user32.dll can have different names in C# than in C++.

