To achieve this, you can use the CSS `space-around` value with `flex-wrap` set to `wrap`. This will distribute the space evenly between the elements as long as they fit within the container, and if there is a gap between the last two elements, it will be half the width of the container. Here is an example:
```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  justify-content: space-around;
}
```
In this example, the gap between the elements will be half the width of the container, and if there are two elements on the last row, they will be positioned next to each other without a gap. If there are three or more elements on the last row, the gap will be present.

Note that if you want to control the gap size explicitly, you can use the `space-between` value and set the container's `width` to be the sum of the widths of its child elements plus the desired gap size. For example:
```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  justify-content: space-between;
  width: 100%;
}

.container > * {
  width: calc(20% - 10px); /* 80% total width minus 10px gap */
}
```
In this example, the container's width is set to 100% and the gap size is set to 10 pixels. The child elements' widths are set to 80% of the container width, which means they will take up 80% of the total width minus the gap size. This will result in a gap of 10 pixels between the elements.

