Here's a simple example of a Supabase edge function in TypeScript that acts as a webhook for a Discord bot:

```
import { ServerlessService, EdgeFunction } from "@supabase/supabase-js";
import DiscordWebhook from "discord.js";

export class DiscordWebhookEdgeFunction extends ServerlessService {
  constructor(private supabase: Supabase) {
    super({
      service: "DiscordWebhookEdgeFunction",
      handler: this.handleRequest.bind(this),
    });
  }

  async handleRequest(req: Request, res: Response) {
    const payload = req.body;
    const webhook = new DiscordWebhook("WEBHOOK_ID", "WEBHOOK_TOKEN");
    const message = await webhook.send(payload.content);
    res.set("Content-Type", "text/plain");
    await res.write(message);
    await res.end();
  }
}
```

In this example, we're creating a new edge function that extends the `ServerlessService` class from `@supabase/supabase-js`. We're also importing the `DiscordWebhook` class from `discord.js`, which we'll use to send webhook messages to Discord.
The `DiscordWebhookEdgeFunction` class has a constructor that sets up the service and sets the handler method. The `handleRequest` method is the handler that will be called when a request is made to the edge function. It retrieves the payload from the request, creates a new Discord webhook instance using the appropriate ID and token, and sends the webhook message with the payload's content.
Note that you'll need to replace `"WEBHOOK_ID"` and `"WEBHOOK_TOKEN"` with the actual webhook ID and token for your Discord server.

Here is an example of a server-side app using TypeScript, Node.js, Express, and TypeORM that has 3 models: a User model, a Bet model, and a Reward model. The User model has a name, email address, and an array of Bets. The Bet model has a name, description, quantity, a reference to a User, and a single Reward. The Reward model has a name and belongs to a Bet.

User.ts:
```
import { Entity, Column, PrimaryGeneratedColumn } from 'typeorm';

@Entity()
export class User {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
  id: number;

  @Column()
  name: string;

  @Column()
  email: string;

  @OneToMany(() => Bet, (bet) => bet.user)
  bets: Bet[];
}
```
Bet.ts:
```
import { Entity, Column, OneToMany, ManyToOne } from 'typeorm';

@Entity()
export class Bet {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
  id: number;

  @Column()
  name: string;

  @Column()
  description: string;

  @OneToMany(() => Reward, (reward) => reward.bet)
  rewards: Reward[];

  @ManyToOne(() => User, (user) => user.bets)
  user: User;
}
```
Reward.ts:
```
import { Entity, Column, BelongsToOne } from 'typeorm';

@Entity()
export class Reward {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
  id: number;

  @Column()
  name: string;

  @Column()
  description: string;

  @BelongsToOne(() => Bet, (bet) => bet.rewards)
  bet: Bet;
}
```
In this example, the User model has a one-to-many relationship with the Bet model, and the Bet model has a one-to-many relationship with the Reward model. The Reward model has a one-to-one relationship with the Bet model.

