To make an AJAX request from jQuery, you can use the `$.ajax()` method. Here's an example of how to use it to send a GET request to an API:
```css
$.ajax({
  url: 'https://example.com/api/endpoint',
  method: 'GET',
  dataType: 'json',
  success: function(data) {
    // do something with the returned data
  },
  error: function(jqXHR, textStatus, errorThrown) {
    // handle error
  }
});
```
In this example, `url` is the URL of the API endpoint you want to request, `method` is the HTTP method you want to use (in this case, GET), `dataType` is the expected data type of the response (in this case, JSON), and `success` and `error` are functions that will be called if the request is successful or not, respectively.

You can also pass in additional options as key-value pairs, such as `data` for sending data in the request body, `headers` for sending headers, and `statusCode` for handling specific HTTP status codes.

